{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Correction to my previous comment — the forward-reference concern was wrong\n\nMy earlier sweep raised this:\n\n> the plan's insert point (\"after `_discover_project_config` `:217`\") is now `:166`, but `raw_layers` also calls `user_config_path`, at `:250` — i.e. *after* the proposed insertion.\n\n**That is a false finding. Retract it.** Two errors compounded into a hazard that does not exist.\n\n**There is no function named `raw_layers`.** The caller is `_ordered_layers`, defined at `config.py:249`.\n\n**`:250` is not a call site — it is the definition line, in a different file.** `user_config_path` is defined at `src/rebar/_config_sources.py:250`. I read that number off the definition and reported it as a call in `config.py`, which is how a cross-file line number turned into an ordering problem.\n\nChecked directly: within `_config_sources.py`, `user_config_path` appears on **exactly one line** — `:250`, its own `def`. Nothing in that module calls it, so inserting a helper after `_discover_project_config` (`:166`) cannot create a forward reference. The real call sites are all in other modules, which import the symbol; Python resolves those at call time regardless of definition order.\n\nThis is the fifth citation error of mine in this epic, and the mechanism is the same each time: reading a line number out of one tool's output and attaching it to a different file. The check that would have caught it costs one command — print the function enclosing each hit rather than the hit alone.\n\n---\n\n### What the re-measurement does establish\n\nThe duplication this story targets is **real**, but it is duplication of *access paths*, not of the definition. One definition is reached three different ways:\n\n| path | call sites |\n|---|---|\n| `config.py:48` re-export, called bare | `config.py:260` (`_ordered_layers`), `:353` (`_resolve`), `:582` (`read_reserved_section`) |\n| `_config.user_config_path()` | `_commands/show_config.py:44`, `:90` |\n| `_config_sources.user_config_path()` via a function-local import at `:110` | `_config_resolvers.py:114` |\n\n7 call sites, 3 modules, 3 spellings of the same function. That is a defensible target, and it is a better framing for Context/Problem than the line-count argument the plan currently leads with — which, as recorded earlier, rests on `config.py` being at the 800-line cap when it is **624 lines**.\n\nStill outstanding from the earlier sweep, unchanged by this correction:\n\n- **AC4 is vacuous.** \"At most 778 lines\" is already true of the untouched tree at 624, so an empty diff passes it.\n- **AC1's expected post-change hit set is misstated** — it claims a hit should remain inside `_config_probe_paths`, and no hit is there.\n- The 14 drifted line citations.\n\nI also checked this story for supersession by a landed sibling, since that is what I just found on `cb2b-7446-748c-47bc`: **e123 is not superseded.** All 7 call sites and all 3 access paths are live on `origin/main` `403719c01ce`."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1793423533538657282,"uuid":"9309103f-ea77-4952-a99f-ad1366740d9e"}